## The earlier failure

An earlier exported version of the port failed the synthetic-v1 comparison
on seven side-effect answers. The runner and grader ran on
Windows x64 with Node v24.22.1 and npm 11.19.1, after the locked dependencies
were installed offline with install scripts disabled and the package was
built. No retry was used, and no live service or real deployed deck was
contacted. Dependency packages came from the npm cache;
the test answers themselves were executed, not cached.

That run's tallies: rendering 1754 pass, 0 fail; semantics 2389 pass, 0 fail;
persistence 16 pass, 0 fail; side effects 12 pass, 7 fail. The grader also
reported 8 of 8 read-write checks and 12 of 12 other-table write checks
passing. Normalization was N1, N2 and N3. Scenario render and ask answers
overlap the category totals, so the categories must not be summed into one
coverage number. The grade exited with status 1.

All seven failing answers differed at the same place. The port built an
unsubscribe link ending in `&success%5Bredirect%5D=soft`; the recorded Ruby
answer ends before that suffix. The grader neither declared nor masked the
difference.

Affected steps:

- create-topic-with-fields-as-admin#1
- update-content-adds-link#1
- rename-referenced-tag#1
- delete-note#1
- delete-topic-with-fields#1
- create-compound-on-new-left#1
- update-then-render#1

A later version was checked the same way from a source-only package, using an
already installed dependency tree with nothing downloaded or installed. Its
build, a card render, the local health and Home pages, and an orderly shutdown
all passed. The grader refused a full grade because it requires an exact
Git commit, which the source-only run did not supply, so it was run in its
diagnostic mode, with checker format decko-byte-checker/1. It reported the same category counts and the
same seven link mismatches, with the verdict diagnostic-fail. No expectation,
strict setting or engine behaviour was changed to hide them. A diagnostic run
is not a full grade.

## The correction

The seven differences were the port's own success-envelope suffix on the
unsubscribe link, and nothing else. The reference's view posts no success
envelope, so the port now posts none either, and the link carries the
reference's bytes in every configuration (src/card/notify/follow-mail-views.ts).

A targeted test replays the seven affected steps on the synthetic-v1 deck and
compares the mail the engine sends, byte for byte, with the recorded Ruby
answer. The redistributable test is included at
`tests/card/notify/notification-unfollow-link-parity.test.ts`. All seven failed
before the change with exactly the suffix above, and all seven passed after
it. That test checks those seven answers only; the whole-fixture results are
the comparisons under "Latest result".
